\details{

Tsallis entropy is defined as:
\deqn{H_q = \frac{1}{q-1} \left(1 - \sum_{i=1}^N p_i^q \right)}
where \eqn{p_i} are the normalized expression values (relative abundances) of transcripts, and \eqn{q} is the entropic index. For \eqn{q = 1}, the function returns the Shannon entropy:
\deqn{H_1 = -\sum_{i=1}^N p_i \log(p_i)}

Interpretation of q:
\itemize{
  \item \eqn{q < 1}: Emphasizes rare transcripts.
  \item \eqn{q = 1}: Returns Shannon entropy (standard information entropy).
  \item \eqn{q > 1}: Emphasizes common transcripts.
  \item \eqn{q = 2}: Corresponds to quadratic entropy, related to the Gini-Simpson index.
}

If only one transcript is present, the result is NaN. If all expression values are zero, the result is NA.

This function supports vector q: if multiple q values are provided, entropy is calculated for each q.
